"""Author-approved language revision; no changes to underlying analyses."""
REVISIONS = {
2: "Understanding achievement-related factors can inform educational evaluation, but statistical findings are often difficult for non-specialists to interpret. This study examined factors associated with mathematics achievement and developed GRADE-Agent to make the findings accessible through conversation.",
3: "Using the Japanese PISA 2022 sample, we combined weighted predictive modelling, SHAP-based feature attribution, and cross-fitted Double Machine Learning (DML). The findings informed GRADE-Agent (Grade-performance Reasoning, Analysis, Diagnosis and Explanation Agent), which gathers contextual information and offers brief, provisional learning-support summaries.",
4: "Mathematics self-efficacy showed the clearest convergence between predictive contribution and a stable positive DML-adjusted association; disciplinary climate also showed a stable positive adjusted association. Support-related findings required contextual interpretation. Two teachers independently rated 120 English final replies from GRADE-Agent and an ordinary multi-turn LLM across twelve synthetic cases and five runs per system. GRADE-Agent received higher mean ratings on all four dimensions. Its largest advantage concerned suggestion appropriateness and actionability (4.75 versus 3.61 on a five-point scale; paired difference 1.14, 95% case-cluster bootstrap CI 0.86–1.45). Agreement between teachers varied across dimensions.",
5: "These findings provide preliminary support for translating achievement-related research into conversational educational support. They indicate higher teacher-rated reply quality under the study conditions, without establishing individual diagnostic accuracy or improved teaching decisions or student achievement. Further evaluation requires new cases, more teachers, and authentic educational use.",
8: "Understanding factors associated with academic achievement helps educators interpret differences in student learning. Achievement scores describe outcomes, but students with similar scores may differ in their learning beliefs, family resources, classroom experiences, and access to educational opportunities. Examining these conditions can identify issues that warrant attention in educational evaluation and support planning. Their practical interpretation requires a distinction between statistical relationships and established causes, particularly when discussing an individual learner.",
9: "Machine learning increasingly informs research on student performance in large-scale assessments. Martínez-Abad et al. (2020) used educational data mining to investigate school effectiveness in PISA, while Wang et al. (2026) reviewed 57 studies of international large-scale assessments. That review identified applications in feature selection and prediction, alongside a need for stronger theoretical grounding and methodological rigour. Related studies examined ICT resources and engagement in PISA (Wang & Wang, 2023) and behavioural predictors in online peer assessment (Jin et al., 2024). These applications identify informative patterns, but predictive importance does not establish a factor's adjusted relationship with achievement. Prediction and explanation address distinct questions (Shmueli, 2010).",
10: "Explainable artificial intelligence helps researchers examine how models use student and contextual characteristics. Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP) attributes a model's predictions to its input features (Lundberg & Lee, 2017). Feature importance remains conditional on the model and data, including shared information among predictors. A highly ranked feature may therefore represent a proxy relationship, rather than an independent influence or an appropriate intervention target. Educational interpretation requires distinguishing predictive contribution from relationships estimated after adjustment for observed covariates.",
11: "Double Machine Learning (DML) provides a complementary perspective by estimating a focal parameter while adjusting for observed covariates (Chernozhukov et al., 2018). In this study, it examines prespecified factors alongside the predictive analysis. Causal interpretation still depends on assumptions about confounding, temporal ordering, overlap, and measurement. These assumptions require caution in cross-sectional educational surveys, where attitudes, support, and achievement may influence one another. We therefore interpret the estimates as adjusted associations under the specified models.",
12: "A further challenge concerns how non-specialists interpret and use these findings. Predictive metrics, feature rankings, and DML estimates convey different information and require explanation. Population-level associations also need contextual consideration before they inform discussion of an individual learner. Relevant information includes the timing of events, alternative explanations, and facts that remain unknown. An accessible educational application must preserve these distinctions while helping users explore possible support.",
13: "Conversational AI offers one way to make research findings accessible through natural-language interaction. Within educational evaluation, Lee (2023) integrated AI-enabled evaluation into student-generated feedback in a large-scale online course. Such applications demonstrate opportunities for supporting evaluation processes, while raising questions about output quality. Fluent replies alone do not ensure faithful use of research findings or appropriate communication of uncertainty. An educational-support application therefore needs both a defined analytical basis and an evaluation of its replies.",
14: "Related work used an intelligent-agent framework to examine mathematics relative learning efficiency (Hu et al., in press). That study assessed performance relative to observed learning conditions. Here, achievement itself is the analytical outcome, followed by an evaluation of conversational learning-support replies. The studies share an interest in connecting educational analysis with intelligent agents, but address different analytical targets and evaluation questions.",
15: "We first examined mathematics achievement in the Japanese sample of the Programme for International Student Assessment (PISA) 2022. Weighted prediction and SHAP characterised predictive contributions, while DML estimated adjusted associations for prespecified factors. The analyses used the mathematics plausible values, as detailed in Methods (OECD, 2024). Japan provides a defined educational context for the initial analysis and application; the estimated relationships are not assumed to generalise across education systems.",
16: "We then developed GRADE-Agent (Grade-performance Reasoning, Analysis, Diagnosis and Explanation Agent) to present and discuss these findings through natural-language interaction. The platform relates research findings to user-supplied circumstances and possible learning-support responses. Its role is to support interpretation, without introducing a new causal estimator or independently establishing the causes of an individual student's achievement. Where case information is incomplete, explanations remain provisional.",
17: "We compared GRADE-Agent with an ordinary multi-turn LLM using the same model backbone, staged case information, and generation settings. Twelve researcher-constructed cases and five runs per system yielded 120 English final replies. Two teachers independently rated contextual relevance, consistency with case facts, suggestion appropriateness and actionability, and clarity using randomised anonymous materials. This complete-system comparison evaluates reply quality, not the contribution of individual components or improvements in teaching and learning. The cases were development-known rather than an unseen test set.",
19: "RQ1. Which characteristics contribute to mathematics achievement prediction, and which prespecified factors show adjusted associations with achievement under the DML models?",
20: "RQ2. How can predictive and DML findings inform conversational access to, and appropriately bounded interpretation of, achievement-related factors through GRADE-Agent?",
21: "RQ3. How do teachers rate GRADE-Agent and an ordinary multi-turn LLM on contextual relevance, consistency with case facts, suggestion appropriateness and actionability, and clarity?",
22: "The study makes two connected contributions. First, it combines predictive modelling, SHAP, and DML to distinguish predictive contributions from adjusted associations within a defined educational context. This contribution concerns their integrated application, rather than a new estimator. Second, it develops GRADE-Agent and compares the educational quality of its replies with those of an ordinary multi-turn LLM. Together, the analyses examine achievement-related factors and their communication for educational discussion, while retaining the distinction between population-level findings and individual judgement.",
25: "The conceptual framework distinguishes three analytical roles. Predictive modelling examines how student and contextual characteristics jointly predict achievement (Albreiki et al., 2021; Alnasyan et al., 2024). SHAP describes their contributions within a fitted model (Lundberg & Lee, 2017). DML examines a prespecified factor's adjusted association with achievement (Chernozhukov et al., 2018). These roles provide complementary information for educational interpretation.",
26: "A strong predictive contribution indicates that a characteristic helps distinguish achievement patterns within the model. An adjusted association describes its relationship with achievement under specified assumptions and covariate adjustment. The findings need not coincide, and neither alone establishes the benefit of an intervention that changes the factor.",
27: "These distinctions guide how findings enter educational discussion. Predictive rankings describe model use, while DML estimates describe adjusted associations for independently prespecified factors. Interpretation retains the uncertainty and population-level scope of the analysis.",
29: "Formative assessment provides an educational basis for diagnostic support. It involves collecting and interpreting learning information to determine appropriate next steps, beyond classifying or summarising performance (Black & Wiliam, 2009). From this perspective, achievement data become useful when they contribute to understanding learning needs and considering educational responses.",
30: "The same achievement score may reflect different learning experiences, conditions, or support needs. A decline may indicate persistent difficulty, a temporary change, or incomplete information about recent learning. Interpretation therefore requires contextual information and consideration of alternative explanations. Teacher diagnostic competence similarly connects observations of students' circumstances and learning behaviour with interpretations that inform instructional decisions (Klug et al., 2013).",
31: "Diagnostic support should accordingly allow interpretations to change as information develops. It should distinguish reported facts from hypotheses and identify what remains unknown. Where information is insufficient, retaining uncertainty is preferable to assigning a fixed explanation.",
32: "A human-centred approach preserves educators' responsibility for decisions about individual learners. Educational AI research emphasises transparency, stakeholder participation, and careful evaluation of outputs that may shape learner support (Bond et al., 2024; Khosravi et al., 2022; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). Suggestions should therefore be understandable and open to review. The system assists professional judgement, while teachers and other relevant stakeholders decide which actions fit the circumstances.",
34: "Contextual inquiry connects achievement-related findings with educational support. Empirical analysis identifies factors worth considering, while learner information establishes which questions are relevant. An association involving teacher support, for example, prompts questions about when support began and which difficulties preceded it. The association alone cannot establish support's role in that learner's performance.",
35: "Conversation allows users to clarify circumstances and revise initial interpretations (Chiu et al., 2023; Kuhail et al., 2023; Yan et al., 2024). In GRADE-Agent, research findings guide areas for attention, while case information informs discussion of possible support. A brief summary connects available facts with unresolved questions and possible next steps. Teachers remain responsible for checking the interpretation and judging whether suggested actions suit the learner.",
36: "The basis of a suggestion and its educational quality require separate consideration. Relevant research does not ensure that a reply addresses case facts accurately or offers understandable advice. The framework therefore distinguishes contextual relevance, consistency with case facts, suggestion appropriateness and actionability, and clarity. Teacher ratings assess these aspects of reply quality, without establishing improved student outcomes.",
37: "Figure 1 connects the three stages. Empirical analysis establishes findings and their interpretive scope; the conversational application relates them to described learning circumstances; teacher evaluation examines the resulting replies. Each stage informs the next while supporting a distinct type of claim.",
41: "The study combined empirical analysis with the development and evaluation of GRADE-Agent. Weighted prediction and SHAP examined predictive contributions to mathematics achievement, while DML estimated adjusted associations for 12 prespecified factors. The findings informed conversational discussion of learning needs and support options. A separate comparison with an ordinary multi-turn LLM assessed final-reply quality using synthetic cases and independent teacher ratings.",
43: "We used student questionnaire and mathematics assessment data from PISA 2022 (OECD, 2023a, 2023b). Education systems differ in curricula, school organisation, socioeconomic composition, language, and access to digital resources. These differences may affect both student characteristics and their associations with achievement. Pooling systems without adequate contextual adjustment could therefore complicate interpretation.",
44: "We selected Japan as a defined educational context for examining these associations and developing the initial GRADE-Agent evidence base.",
45: "Restricting the data to Japan (CNT = JPN) and excluding records without the required mathematics outcome yielded 5,760 students. We used the final student weight (W_FSTUWT) for descriptive statistics, predictive evaluation, and DML estimation. Although the data included 80 replicate weights, we did not implement replicate-weight variance estimation. School-clustered standard errors account for within-school dependence but not the full uncertainty from PISA's complex sampling design (OECD, 2024).",
50: "Mathematics achievement was represented by ten plausible values (PV1MATH–PV10MATH). Their mean (MATH_MEAN) served only descriptive reporting. We conducted predictive and DML analyses separately for each plausible value and summarised the estimates across all ten analyses. This procedure avoids treating the plausible-value mean as a directly observed individual score.",
51: "We specified 12 candidate factors before examining predictive performance or SHAP rankings. Selection reflected their educational relevance, coverage of learning environments, and availability as measurable PISA 2022 constructs. The aim was to represent conditions relevant to educational discussion, rather than select factors based on predictive performance.",
58: "These domains provide a multidimensional account of students' learning circumstances. The candidate set remained fixed throughout the predictive and DML analyses, independent of the SHAP results.",
59: "DML adjustment included sex, age, language, parental education and occupation, student–teacher relations, the remaining candidate factors, and other retained background variables specified in the analytical pipeline.",
61: "Preprocessing retained usable observations while limiting information leakage between training and evaluation data.",
62: "We restricted the data to Japan and excluded records without the required mathematics outcome. Variables with more than 50% missing values were removed, including FEELSAFE, REPEAT, IMMIG, and SCHRISK in the filtered analytical file.",
63: "We imputed missing numerical values using medians, imputed categorical values, and then one-hot encoded categorical variables. Numerical inputs to linear prediction models were standardised. Fitted preprocessing used only the relevant training data or estimation folds, excluding held-out observations.",
64: "Prediction used an 80% training and 20% held-out test split (random_state = 42), shared across all ten plausible-value analyses. We fitted imputation, encoding, and other preprocessing on training data before applying them to the test set. DML fitted preprocessing and nuisance models within the cross-fitting procedure to limit leakage between estimation folds.",
65: "Weighted analyses used the final student weight, maintaining a common basis for model comparisons and adjusted-association estimation.",
67: "We compared Random Forest, ExtraTrees, RidgeCV, ElasticNetCV, and LightGBM using the same 42-variable feature set and training–test split for each plausible value. Five-fold cross-validation selected LightGBM hyperparameters.",
69: "We summarised performance across ten plausible-value analyses. The standard deviations describe variation across these analyses, not full sampling uncertainty under PISA's complex design.",
71: "We calculated SHAP values for the selected prediction model using a weighted sample of up to 3,000 training cases per plausible value. Mean absolute SHAP values were calculated within each analysis and summarised across the ten plausible values.",
72: "Larger SHAP values identify stronger contributions to the fitted prediction function. They indicate predictive information, without establishing that a factor causes achievement differences.",
73: "SHAP rankings did not determine the DML candidate set. The two analyses addressed predictive contribution and adjusted associations separately.",
75: "We applied DML separately to each of the 12 pre-specified candidate factors using a partially linear model (Chernozhukov et al., 2018):",
81: "Standard errors were clustered by school identifier (CNTSCHID). We did not implement PISA's replicate-weight variance estimation, so these standard errors do not capture the complete complex-sampling uncertainty.",
82: "Causal interpretation requires conditional exchangeability, sufficient overlap, a consistent focal-factor definition, appropriate temporal ordering, and adequate nuisance-function estimation. Cross-sectional observations alone cannot establish these assumptions. Attitudes, school experiences, support, and achievement may be measured simultaneously, leaving their temporal relationships uncertain. We therefore interpret DML estimates as population-level adjusted associations under the specified assumptions.",
84: "We organised the predictive, SHAP, and DML findings in an empirical evidence knowledge base for GRADE-Agent. The summaries made the findings available for discussion of students' learning circumstances.",
85: "Each factor summary recorded predictive contribution, the DML estimate where available, uncertainty, and interpretive limits. These records distinguished predictive information from adjusted associations and preserved the boundary between population-level findings and individual explanations.",
86: "The summaries informed areas for further inquiry, questions about missing contextual information, and discussion of possible learning-support options. Their use depended on the information provided about the learner.",
87: "The knowledge base thus supplied the analytical basis for conversational support, with contextual inquiry guiding its interpretation in particular learning situations.",
89: "The research team constructed cases without using real student records. Development included eight initial cases and twelve further synthetic cases covering achievement, confidence, independent work, instructional language, resources, support timing, conflicting or missing information, and safeguarding. The present comparison reused the twelve-case set following prototype development. These cases were development-known, not held out.",
90: "Each case comprised four fixed English user turns. The first three supplied facts in stages; the fourth requested one natural paragraph of approximately 80–140 words on priorities and initial support options. Both systems received identical staged facts, including initial safety information where specified. Later facts did not adapt to either system's questions. The design assessed final replies after matched information exposure, rather than adaptive information elicitation.",
91: "The task-specific rubric drew on relevance, factual consistency, and understandable expression in text-generation evaluation (Fabbri et al., 2021; Liu et al., 2023). We added suggestion appropriateness for the educational task. This adapted rubric was neither a validated educational diagnostic scale nor automated G-Eval scoring. Each dimension used integer ratings from 1 to 5, with anchors at 1, 3, and 5. Intermediate scores represented intermediate quality; no weighted total was calculated.",
92: "Contextual relevance assessed attention to issues supported by the case. A rating of 1 indicated missing the main concern or largely irrelevant content; 3 indicated relevant but generic, distracting, or incomplete discussion. A rating of 5 indicated focused discussion without automatically treating neutral background information as a problem.",
93: "Consistency with case facts assessed faithful use of supplied information. A rating of 1 indicated fabrication, key contradictions, or certainty about an unsupported individual cause. A rating of 3 indicated broad consistency with important unsupported inferences or insufficient qualification. A rating of 5 distinguished unknown from absent information and confined interpretation to the case's support.",
94: "Suggestion appropriateness and actionability was the primary outcome. A rating of 1 indicated unsuitable, unsafe, or unusable advice; 3 indicated generally appropriate but insufficiently specific or contextualised suggestions. A rating of 5 indicated feasible, case-sensitive suggestions that respected uncertainty. Further inquiry or protection could merit a high rating when information was insufficient or safety was at issue.",
95: "Clarity assessed whether teachers or parents could follow the reply. A rating of 1 indicated difficult expression or organisation; 3 indicated understandable writing with unnecessary terminology, repetition, or organisational problems. A rating of 5 indicated clear, coherent, concise wording. Contextual relevance, case-fact consistency, and clarity were secondary outcomes. Sections 5.4 and 5.5 describe the evaluation and paired results.",
96: "Two teachers rated every original final reply using independently randomised anonymous A/B materials containing full case facts and English replies. Chinese translations served as reading aids, with instructions to judge the English originals, particularly for clarity. Ratings therefore reflect bilingual evaluation conditions, rather than an independently verified English-only assessment.",
98: "The comparison used an ordinary multi-turn LLM and the complete conversational GRADE-Agent with the same DeepSeek Flash backbone and generation settings. Five conversations per case yielded 60 final replies per system and 120 overall. The frozen protocol designated suggestion appropriateness and actionability as primary; the remaining dimensions were secondary. Earlier report-based component comparisons were not pooled with this experiment.",
99: "Both systems received identical staged information. Each teacher independently assessed all final replies in randomised anonymous A/B materials. A concealed assignment key matched ratings to source replies, avoiding reliance on display labels. Section 5.4 describes the presentation and rating procedure.",
100: "We averaged the two teacher scores for each reply, then the five runs within each case and system. Paired GRADE-Agent-minus-ordinary-LLM differences were calculated for the twelve cases. We estimated 95% percentile bootstrap intervals using 50,000 resamples of paired cases with replacement (seed 20261027). Each selected case retained both systems and all runs. Repeated generations were not treated as independent student cases.",
101: "Intervals are conditional on the two fixed teachers. Secondary-outcome intervals are exploratory, pointwise, and unadjusted for multiple comparisons. Quadratic-weighted Cohen's kappa and exact agreement were calculated separately for each criterion across 120 matched replies. System differences and inter-rater agreement address separate questions.",
102: "The design evaluates final-reply quality under matched information exposure. It does not isolate component effects, test adaptive questioning, or establish diagnostic accuracy or educational outcomes for real students.",
105: "LightGBM performed best using the common 42-variable feature set, with mean weighted R² of 0.501, RMSE of 66.15, and MAE of 52.75. Random Forest and ExtraTrees both achieved weighted R² of 0.469. Their respective RMSE values were 68.24 and 68.28, and MAE values were 54.15 and 54.22. ElasticNet and Ridge achieved weighted R² of 0.388 and 0.387, RMSE of 73.28 and 73.32, and MAE of 57.32 and 57.22, respectively. LightGBM's approximately 0.113 advantage in weighted R² over the regularised linear models indicated additional predictive value from nonlinear structure. Considerable achievement variation nevertheless remained unexplained (Figure 2(a)).",
107: "MATHEFF had the largest mean absolute SHAP value (29.529). Familiarity with mathematical concepts (FAMCON; 9.514) and weekday ICT activity (ICTWKDY; 9.152) followed, ahead of ESCS (7.605) and HOMEPOS (7.026). Cognitive activation, weekend ICT activity, family support, and ICT-based feedback also ranked among the ten largest predictive contributors (Figure 2(b)).",
108: "The rankings captured psychological, socioeconomic, family, classroom, and digital-use information within the fitted model. FAMCON, ICTWKDY, COGACMCO, ICTWKEND, FAMSUP, and ICTFEED were informative predictors but fell outside the 12 prespecified DML factors. We retained them as predictive-context signals without corresponding DML estimates. Among the DML factors, MATHEFF had the largest SHAP contribution, while ESCS, HOMEPOS, and DISCLIM contributed less. Predictive contribution and adjusted association therefore remained distinct forms of information.",
110: "MATHEFF and DISCLIM met the prespecified stable-positive criteria under the weighted analysis with school-clustered standard errors. Pooled estimates were 28.90 (SE = 1.54, p < .001) and 13.74 (SE = 1.90, p < .001), respectively. Each estimate uses its factor's measurement scale, precluding a direct ranking of relative importance. ICTQUAL was positive across ten plausible values, but its pooled estimate of 2.37 (SE = 1.27, p = .063) placed it in the weak or context-sensitive class. ESCS was also positive (16.41, SE = 11.81), without pooled statistical significance (p = .165). These classifications use the prespecified .05 threshold and implemented variance estimates, rather than complete design-based PISA inference.",
111: "BELONG (−5.96, SE = 1.22, p < .001), ICTSCH (−4.52, SE = 1.22, p < .001), and TEACHSUP (−3.24, SE = 1.20, p = .007) showed directionally consistent negative estimates. All three pooled estimates were statistically significant, but their educational meanings required separate contextual interpretation.",
112: "Greater teacher support may reflect assistance following learning difficulties, consistent with earlier PISA interpretations (OECD, 2010). For school ICT resources, availability differs from educational use, which relates to student engagement (Wang & Wang, 2023). Needs-based allocation is another possibility, but the data contain no allocation histories to test it. School belonging concerns perceived membership and acceptance; its negative estimate cannot simply be attributed to greater school attention. The data establish neither these mechanisms nor their temporal order. We therefore assigned all three factors to boundary review, without recommending reduced support, belonging, or ICT provision.",
113: "HOMEPOS, ESCS, ICTQUAL, ICTENQ, and BULLIED entered the weak or context-sensitive class because pooled estimates did not reach the .05 significance threshold. Limited pooled support differs from directional instability: ICTQUAL remained positive across all ten analyses despite its non-significant pooled estimate. ANXMAT and MATHEF21 were directionally unstable because their signs varied across plausible values. Figure 2(c) presents pooled DML estimates and 95% confidence intervals.",
114: "MATHEFF and DISCLIM provided the clearest stable-positive associations under the implemented analysis. BELONG, ICTSCH, and TEACHSUP required contextual interpretation; other factors had limited pooled support or inconsistent directions. These distinctions informed the organisation of findings within GRADE-Agent.",
116: "Figure 2(d) groups the findings into stable positive, boundary review, directionally unstable, and weak or context-sensitive classes. Stable positive summarises the clearest positive adjusted associations. Boundary review identifies negative associations requiring contextual interpretation. The other classes distinguish inconsistent directions from limited pooled statistical support. Supplementary Section S10 provides the factor-level mapping and interpretive limits.",
117: "These classes guide discussion alongside user-supplied information. For mathematics self-efficacy, the application explains the positive population-level association and invites consideration of its relevance to the case. For teacher support, it asks whether additional help preceded or followed learning difficulties. Unstable and weak or context-sensitive findings retain their uncertainty. The organisation supports discussion while separating population-level patterns from individual explanations.",
122: "Research findings become educationally useful when educators can consider their relevance to a learner's circumstances. Knowing which factors are associated with achievement is insufficient for deciding what deserves attention in a particular situation. Interpretation also requires information about learning experiences and existing support.",
123: "GRADE-Agent provides conversational access to the present study's findings on mathematics achievement. It helps users explore achievement-related factors in relation to a described learning situation.",
124: "A positive association involving mathematics self-efficacy can prompt discussion of confidence and approaches to mathematical tasks. A negative teacher-support association instead requires inquiry into the context and timing of assistance. In both cases, research informs questions for discussion, while case information guides possible interpretations.",
125: "The application thus connects empirical findings with educators' questions about learning needs and possible support.",
127: "A decline in achievement or an isolated behaviour rarely provides a complete account of learning difficulty. GRADE-Agent uses multi-turn conversation to gather further information about the learning situation before offering a summary.",
128: "Users first describe an observed difficulty. Follow-up questions explore independent task completion, the onset of difficulties, responses to assistance, and the timing of existing support. These questions help clarify the student's learning experience before an interpretation is offered.",
129: "The agent then summarises issues warranting attention and suggests possible next steps linked to the supplied information. Where important facts remain unclear, the summary identifies questions for further inquiry.",
130: "Research findings and case observations serve complementary roles. The former identify relevant associations; the latter situate their interpretation within the learner's observed performance, experiences, and support arrangements. This combination is intended to support context-sensitive discussion of learning needs.",
132: "The browser interface supports continued conversation about the same learning situation. Users can describe circumstances, add observations, and review a concise summary with possible next steps.",
133: "Natural-language interaction allows users to discuss findings without directly interpreting statistical outputs or specialised terminology. The conversation connects the research with the information supplied about the student.",
138: "Teacher evaluation compared the educational quality of GRADE-Agent's replies with those of an ordinary multi-turn LLM.",
139: "Both systems processed 12 synthetic cases with five runs per case and system, producing 120 English final replies. Matched case information supported comparison under equivalent information conditions.",
140: "Two teachers independently evaluated anonymised A/B pairs containing full case descriptions and original English replies. Pair order and system assignment were randomised separately for each teacher. Chinese translations were available as reading aids.",
146: "All dimensions used a five-point scale. Suggestion appropriateness and actionability was the prespecified primary outcome; the remaining dimensions were secondary measures of reply quality.",
147: "We averaged ratings across teachers, then runs within each case and system, and compared the resulting scores across 12 cases. Analysis reported mean ratings, paired case-level differences, and 95% percentile bootstrap confidence intervals from 50,000 resamples of the 12 paired cases. Quadratic-weighted Cohen's kappa assessed inter-rater agreement separately for each dimension.",
153: "GRADE-Agent received higher mean ratings across all four dimensions, with the largest difference in suggestion appropriateness and actionability.",
156: "For the primary outcome, GRADE-Agent averaged 4.75 versus 3.61 for the ordinary LLM. The paired difference was 1.14 points (95% bootstrap CI [0.86, 1.45]). Case-level mean ratings favoured GRADE-Agent in all 12 cases.",
157: "Teachers therefore judged GRADE-Agent's suggestions more suitable for the presented circumstances and more useful for identifying next steps. The finding concerns the educational quality of replies in the evaluated cases.",
158: "Contextual relevance averaged 4.03 for GRADE-Agent and 3.53 for the ordinary LLM. Consistency with case facts and clarity averaged 4.10 and 4.14, respectively, versus 3.91 and 3.79. The observed advantage therefore varied across dimensions.",
159: "Higher ratings extended to contextual relevance and expression, while the smaller factual-consistency difference indicated a more limited advantage on that dimension.",
160: "Both teachers favoured GRADE-Agent on the primary outcome. Teacher 1 assigned mean ratings of 4.90 versus 3.57, and Teacher 2 assigned 4.60 versus 3.65.",
161: "Quadratic-weighted κ was 0.794 for contextual relevance and 0.797 for suggestion appropriateness and actionability. Corresponding values were 0.178 for consistency with case facts and 0.116 for clarity. Teachers agreed more closely on educational relevance and practical suggestions than on factual consistency and expression. This agreement pattern is distinct from the differences in mean system ratings.",
166: "The ratings provide preliminary support for GRADE-Agent's reply quality under the evaluated conditions, particularly its suggestion appropriateness and actionability.",
167: "Whether these perceived advantages inform support decisions in authentic educational settings requires further study.",
170: "The study connects analysis of achievement-related factors with evaluation of conversational educational support (Figure 1). The empirical analysis identifies predictive patterns and adjusted associations; the application makes them available for discussion. Teacher evaluation then compares final replies with those from an ordinary multi-turn LLM receiving the same information.",
171: "GRADE-Agent uses research findings to guide areas for attention, contextual questions, and the presentation of possible explanations and support options. The intended contribution is to help educators relate analytical findings to a learning situation.",
172: "The empirical findings illustrate the need for differentiated interpretation. MATHEFF showed convergence between predictive contribution and a stable positive DML-adjusted association, and DISCLIM also showed a stable positive adjusted association. HOMEPOS and ICTENQ lacked comparable pooled support for stable adjusted associations. Predictive information therefore did not carry the same interpretive weight for every factor.",
173: "The application reflects these distinctions by retaining caution when associations are uncertain or require further contextual information. Highly ranked predictors are not presented as equally established individual explanations.",
174: "This use of conversation is consistent with formative assessment, where information informs subsequent instructional decisions (Black & Wiliam, 2009). An initial description starts inquiry, with follow-up information allowing interpretations to develop. Such interaction provides a way to organise observations before considering support.",
175: "Both teachers rated GRADE-Agent's suggestions more highly on average, and paired case-level differences favoured it across all twelve cases. This supports a bounded claim about perceived appropriateness and actionability. It does not establish that the suggestions improve learning or identify the causes of an individual difficulty.",
177: "Negative estimates for BELONG, ICTSCH, and TEACHSUP illustrate why statistical findings require educational interpretation. Their directional consistency and pooled significance do not imply that reducing belonging, ICT resources, or teacher support would improve achievement.",
178: "Teacher support may increase after learning difficulties emerge. Greater reported assistance can therefore reflect an existing need, consistent with earlier PISA interpretations of support directed towards lower-achieving students (OECD, 2010). The negative estimate alone cannot distinguish this response from other explanations.",
179: "For school ICT resources, availability differs from effective instructional use. Schools may also allocate resources in response to existing needs. Without allocation histories, the data cannot test these explanations or justify recommending less provision.",
180: "Belonging concerns perceived membership and acceptance, so it requires a separate interpretation. Its negative estimate cannot be explained simply by greater assistance to lower-achieving students. Unmeasured circumstances, reporting patterns, or other contextual differences may contribute, but the cross-sectional data cannot determine which explanation applies.",
181: "DML adjusts for observed covariates under a specified model; it neither establishes temporal order nor rules out unmeasured influences. These findings therefore inform further questions, including when support began and why resources were provided.",
182: "Educational recommendations require this contextual examination, beyond reading the sign of a statistical coefficient. The application encourages inquiry into the reported relationship before considering possible support.",
184: "The comparison suggests that presenting research alongside case information can support educationally relevant discussion. GRADE-Agent received higher mean teacher ratings across all four dimensions, most clearly for suggestion appropriateness and actionability. The pattern is consistent with more context-sensitive recommendations, although the complete-system comparison cannot attribute the difference to a particular component.",
185: "Teachers judged GRADE-Agent's suggestions more suitable for the cases and more useful for identifying next steps. Higher contextual-relevance ratings similarly indicated closer alignment with the presented circumstances. These results emphasise the value of connecting research findings with questions that inform support planning, rather than presenting statistical information in isolation.",
186: "Educational reply quality also depends on faithful interpretation and clear explanation. Although GRADE-Agent had higher mean ratings for factual consistency and clarity, teachers agreed less closely on these dimensions. Higher system ratings and lower inter-rater agreement describe different aspects of the evaluation. Future studies should provide more explicit scoring guidance for these criteria.",
187: "GRADE-Agent offers one approach to relating achievement research to practical support discussions. Studies involving teachers in authentic situations are needed to examine whether these replies contribute to understanding learning needs and planning appropriate support.",
189: "The Japanese PISA 2022 sample provides a defined context for the initial evidence base. Its associations should not be assumed to transfer unchanged to other education systems.",
190: "School organisation, socioeconomic conditions, teaching practices, resource allocation, and self-report patterns may alter the estimated relationships elsewhere. Applying the approach in another setting therefore requires relevant data analysis and reassessment of the evidence base.",
191: "Professional judgement remains essential for individual cases. Teachers may know about curriculum changes, classroom relationships, health, and the timing of support that PISA variables do not capture. This information may change how a population-level finding should be interpreted.",
192: "Teacher ratings evaluated generated replies, not live-interface usability, professional decision quality, or classroom practice. Synthetic-case performance also does not establish diagnostic accuracy for real students.",
193: "Future evaluation should include a larger, more diverse teacher panel, previously unseen cases, and direct observation of system use. Relevant questions include the usefulness of follow-up questions and the comprehensibility of summaries during educational work. Testing effects on support decisions or student outcomes requires a separate study design.",
194: "GRADE-Agent remains a research prototype for educational discussion. Its potential lies in making research easier to consider alongside learner information, while preserving professional responsibility for support decisions.",
196: "Four limitations constrain the interpretation and use of these findings.",
197: "First, cross-sectional PISA 2022 data cannot by themselves establish causal effects on achievement. The variance estimates also omit elements of PISA's complex sampling design. Predictive patterns and adjusted associations should therefore be interpreted within these design and uncertainty limits.",
198: "Second, the Japanese sample defines the initial educational context. Associations may differ across education systems, and self-reported measures may incompletely represent learning circumstances. Cross-system replication should assess the consistency and contextual relevance of the findings.",
199: "Third, the evaluation reused twelve researcher-constructed cases available during development. Five runs per system increased outputs, not independent cases. Fixed facts did not test adaptive questioning, and the complete-system comparison did not isolate DML or other components. The results therefore do not establish performance on unseen students or component-specific effects.",
200: "Fourth, two teachers used an adapted task-specific rubric, not a validated diagnostic instrument. Agreement was limited for factual consistency and clarity, despite higher mean ratings for GRADE-Agent. Chinese reading aids may have influenced interpretation of English replies. Bootstrap intervals are conditional on these teachers, without representing uncertainty across a teacher population. Further studies require independently recruited raters, clearer calibration, and larger unseen case sets before evaluation in authentic settings.",
202: "This study examined factors associated with mathematics achievement among Japanese students in PISA 2022 and developed GRADE-Agent to support discussion of the findings. The framework connects empirical analysis with conversational exploration of learning circumstances and possible support.",
203: "Across twelve synthetic cases, five runs per system, and two independent teachers, GRADE-Agent received higher mean ratings than an ordinary multi-turn LLM on all four criteria. The largest difference concerned suggestions (4.75 versus 3.61 out of 5; paired difference 1.14, 95% case-cluster bootstrap CI 0.86–1.45). Agreement varied across criteria. These findings indicate a preliminary advantage in teacher-rated reply quality, without establishing individual diagnostic accuracy or improved educational outcomes.",
204: "GRADE-Agent makes achievement-related research available for discussion alongside students' circumstances. Its educational usefulness now requires evaluation of how teachers use these conversations and whether they inform support planning in authentic settings.",
206: "PISA 2022 public-use data and documentation are available from the OECD PISA database. We used Japan's student questionnaire data and ten mathematics plausible values. No personally identifying data were used.",
208: "Supplementary Software 1 contains the analysis software. Supplementary Evaluation Materials contains the frozen English comparison protocol, original replies, anonymous teacher scores, case-level data, and analysis and plotting code. These materials concern the conversational evaluation and are distinct from earlier report-based records. PISA 2022 microdata are not redistributed and remain available from the OECD database.",
210: "The authors used an AI-assisted writing tool for translation, language editing, structural revision, and software prototyping. The authors reviewed all empirical results, interpretations, references, figures, and final wording and retain responsibility for the manuscript."
}
